      <Section>
        <h3>Ormos</h3>

        <IframeBrowser src="https://www.ormos.dev" />

        <p>
          <a target="_blank" href="https://www.ormos.dev">Ormos</a>
          is a personal remote-access tool. An agent runs on a machine you own and
          makes a single outbound connection to a hosted relay, so a browser
          anywhere can open a live terminal on that machine or view a local port
          in an iframe. The agent never opens a listening socket, and one
          multiplexed WebSocket carries every terminal and every proxied port.
        </p>

        <div class={styles.coreTech}>
          <p>Core technologies:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Go</li>
            <li>WebSockets</li>
            <li>Bubble Tea</li>
            <li>PocketBase</li>
            <li>Expo</li>
            <li>Docker</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>
          Checkout the code on
          <a target="_blank" href="https://github.com/nicodes/ormos">
            GitHub<span>!</span>
          </a>
        </p>
      </Section>

      <Section>
        <h3>Komizo</h3>

        <IframeBrowser src="https://www.komizo.dev" />

        <p>
          <a target="_blank" href="https://www.komizo.dev">Komizo</a>
          deploys apps to your own server straight from GitHub Actions. A terminal
          interface sets the box up over SSH, gives each app its own deploy
          account and its own two privileged commands, and puts one shared proxy
          in front of everything. Nothing is installed by hand, and almost
          nothing lives on the server at all.
        </p>

        <div class={styles.coreTech}>
          <p>Core technologies:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Go</li>
            <li>Bubble Tea</li>
            <li>SSH</li>
            <li>Docker</li>
            <li>Caddy</li>
            <li>GitHub Actions</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>
          Checkout the code on
          <a target="_blank" href="https://github.com/nicodes/komizo">
            GitHub<span>!</span>
          </a>
        </p>
      </Section>

      <Section>
        <h3>ClearImg</h3>

        <IframeBrowser src="https://www.clearimg.ai" />

        <p>
          <a target="_blank" href="https://www.clearimg.ai">ClearImg</a>
          turns AI-generated art into transparent sprite assets. Each sprite is
          generated twice in one image — once over white, once over black — and
          reconciling that pair recovers the alpha channel, no manual cutouts
          required. It ships as an API, a CLI, and an app.
        </p>

        <div class={styles.coreTech}>
          <p>Core technologies:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Go</li>
            <li>Gin</li>
            <li>PocketBase</li>
            <li>OpenAI API</li>
            <li>Expo</li>
            <li>Docker</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </Section>

      <Section>
        <h3>Tone Split</h3>

        <IframeBrowser src="https://www.tonesplit.com" />

        <p>
          <a target="_blank" href="https://www.tonesplit.com">Tone Split</a>
          is a stereo synthesizer. Stack oscillators, give each one a waveform, a
          level and a place between hard left and hard right, and watch both
          channels on a scope while you move them. A library of 93 chord voicings
          is a place to start rather than a preset — every voice stays
          independent. There's no backend at all; the audio graph is built on the
          device.
        </p>

        <div class={styles.coreTech}>
          <p>Core technologies:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Expo</li>
            <li>React Native</li>
            <li>TypeScript</li>
            <li>Web Audio API</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </Section>

      <Section>
        <h3>CT Calc</h3>

        <IframeBrowser src="https://www.ctcalc.com" />

        <p>
          <a target="_blank" href="https://www.ctcalc.com">CT Calc</a>
          is used to provide contact time inactivation of Giardia and viruses for
          several disinfectants. These calculations can be useful for determining
          compliance with EPA guidelines.
        </p>

        <div class={styles.coreTech}>
          <p>Core technologies:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Astro</li>
            <li>HTMX</li>
            <li>TypeScript</li>
            <li>Python</li>
            <li>Vercel</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>
          Checkout the code on
          <a target="_blank" href="https://github.com/nicodes/ctcalc">
            GitHub<span>!</span>
          </a>
        </p>
      </Section>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="right">
      <Section>
        <h3>Avior Studio</h3>

        <IframeBrowser src="https://www.avior.studio" />

        <p>
          <a target="_blank" href="https://www.avior.studio">Avior Studio</a>
          is my game studio, and its site is the index to everything under it:
          the developer tools on one side and the games on the other. It's three
          static pages that ship no JavaScript at all, and every product on it is
          either linkable or explicitly marked as not — a status dot is filled
          only for something you can actually run today.
        </p>

        <div class={styles.coreTech}>
          <p>Core technologies:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Astro</li>
            <li>TypeScript</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>
          Checkout the code on
          <a target="_blank" href="https://github.com/aviorstudio/aviorstudio-web">
            GitHub<span>!</span>
          </a>
        </p>
      </Section>

      <Section>
        <h3>GDAM</h3>

        <IframeBrowser src="https://www.gdam.dev" />

        <p>
          <a target="_blank" href="https://www.gdam.dev">GDAM</a>
          is the Godot Addon Manager: install, link, remove and publish Godot
          addons from a small CLI backed by a public registry. Addons come from
          GitHub release assets, publishing is authenticated with an owner-scoped
          key, and a local addon can be linked in place while you're still
          building it.
        </p>

        <div class={styles.coreTech}>
          <p>Core technologies:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Go</li>
            <li>Astro</li>
            <li>PocketBase</li>
            <li>Godot</li>
            <li>Docker</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>
          Checkout the code on
          <a target="_blank" href="https://github.com/aviorstudio/gdam">
            GitHub<span>!</span>
          </a>
        </p>
      </Section>

      <Section>
        <h3>gdlint</h3>

        <IframeBrowser src="https://www.gdlint.dev" />

        <p>
          <a target="_blank" href="https://www.gdlint.dev">gdlint</a>
          is a project-local GDScript hygiene checker for Godot projects. It finds
          the things that quietly accumulate — unused functions and signals, stray
          debug prints, orphaned UID files, missing return types — and it's
          deliberately not an architecture checker, so it stays out of the way of
          how you've chosen to lay a project out.
        </p>

        <div class={styles.coreTech}>
          <p>Core technologies:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Go</li>
            <li>GDScript</li>
            <li>Godot</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>
          Checkout the code on
          <a target="_blank" href="https://github.com/aviorstudio/gdlint">
            GitHub<span>!</span>
          </a>
        </p>
      </Section>

      <Section>
        <h3>termcade</h3>

        <IframeBrowser src="https://www.termca.de" />

        <p>
          <a target="_blank" href="https://www.termca.de">termcade</a>
          is an arcade that runs in your terminal, drawing at sub-cell resolution
          with block pixels. No game is compiled into it — each one is a sandboxed
          WebAssembly package, so the bundled starter pack and anything you write
          against the SDK are installed the exact same way.
        </p>

        <div class={styles.coreTech}>
          <p>Core technologies:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Go</li>
            <li>Bubble Tea</li>
            <li>WebAssembly</li>
            <li>PocketBase</li>
            <li>Docker</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>
          Checkout the code on
          <a target="_blank" href="https://github.com/aviorstudio/termcade">
            GitHub<span>!</span>
          </a>
        </p>
      </Section>

      <Section>
        <h3>Schlop Stop</h3>

        <IframeBrowser src="https://www.schlopstop.com" />

        <p>
          <a target="_blank" href="https://www.schlopstop.com">Schlop Stop</a>
          is a food truck based in Boulder CO. I used AI tools to design the logo
          and built this site to host their menu. It also includes Google Drive support
          to update the menu in real time. More additions are cooking up!
        </p>

        <div class={styles.coreTech}>
          <p>Core technologies:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Astro</li>
            <li>TypeScript</li>
            <li>Google Drive API</li>
            <li>Vercel</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>
          Checkout the code on
          <a target="_blank" href="https://github.com/nicodes/schlopstop">
            GitHub<span>!</span>
          </a>
        </p>
      </Section>

